 1 big thing: Now arriving ... 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 An F-16 Block 70 lands at an air base near Taitung in Taiwan. Photo: Wang Jinfu/Taiwan Military News Agency

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A handful of new F-16 Block 70s have arrived in Taiwan from the U.S. It's been years in the making — and dozens more are outstanding.

 Why it matters: The delivery of these Lockheed Martin-made fighter jets comes as the island's political parties scrap over what to spend on which weapons and why, and as Chinese belligerence hardens regional resolve.

 Their arrival also marks progress in relieving a $30 billion backlog of weapons Taipei sought from Washington. But slipping schedules raise broader questions about whether the arms delivered today will be effective tomorrow. What they're saying: "The defense wonk angle here is 'It took a long time,' with the challenges the defense industrial base is facing" and the demands of conflicts elsewhere , one former Pentagon official told me.

 "Something's getting across the finish line, or at least starting to get across the finish line, and I think that's worth noting," the official added. Still, "Five is great, but that's just a fraction of what was promised." Catch up quick: Two batches of F-16s — three this month and two in October — have landed so far at an air base near Taitung. A total of 66 are on order.

 National Security Council Secretary-General Joseph Wu on Nov. 8 shared footage of the approaching warplanes on X , saying: "Welcome home!" Follow the money: The U.S. approved a multibillion-dollar sale of F-16s to Taiwan in 2019, with deliveries expected to begin in 2023.

 A Lockheed spokesperson told me the company is "committed to delivering mission-critical deterrence capabilities that support U.S. policy objectives and Taiwan's security goals," but referred questions about schedule and potential usage of the jets to the respective governments. Threat level: China considers Taiwan a renegade province and seeks to bring it under its control. U.S. military officials have warned China could attempt a takeover as soon as next year .

 A spokesperson at Taiwan's de facto U.S. embassy told me maintaining the "status quo in the Taiwan Strait remains Taiwan's unwavering goal" and the "ability to procure and receive up-to-date equipment" like the F-16 Block 70 is an "essential part of achieving peace through strength." "Facing a growing challenge," the spokesperson added, "we will continue to strengthen our national defense accordingly, and deepen cooperation with like-minded partners in our region." Zoom in: The U.S. has for decades armed Taiwan, angering China . But holdups have been a sore spot in the relationship — and served as red meat for the Beijing-friendly Kuomintang party.

 The political climate in Taipei "is such that the opposition KMT has tried to use these delays to argue about the unreliability of procurement from the United States and to try to poke holes in President [Lai Ching-te's] request for new special budgets," David Sacks at the Council on Foreign Relations told me. "Their narrative is, 'If the United States can't deliver what we've already paid for, why should we spend more money on things that we're not sure will ever be delivered?'" What we're watching: If and when the Trump administration releases to Taiwan a $14 billion weapons package lawmakers greenlit almost a year ago.

 Go deeper: Taiwan frets as U.S. withholds weapons 

 
 
