 2. Exclusive: Chunmoo trial 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The Chunmoo test, featuring the Once in a Lifetime ship in the Gulf of America. Photo: Courtesy of Hanwha Defense USA

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Hanwha Defense USA alongside its parent Hanwha Aerospace on Sunday test-fired a Chunmoo missile from a vessel off the Mississippi coast.

 Why it matters: The U.S. Navy is hungry for modular, affordable weapons it can drop onto and pull off warships, manned or unmanned.

 This is the first time a Chunmoo system — typically used on land — has been brought to the U.S. Driving the news: One photo from the test I reviewed showed a four-missile pod launcher aimed over the water, off the back of The Spaceport Company's Once in a Lifetime ship. Other images showed the munition exiting and arcing over the Gulf of America .

 Zoom in: The trial, in the works for 11 months, according to executives, gave Hanwha a close look at:

 The effects of firing a missile from a smaller ship; The design demands of containerized or palletized launchers; Fire-control needs. What they're saying: "I'm big believer of crawl, walk, run. I hate doing stunts just to do a stunt. I want to have something that I'm learning from," executive vice president for missiles Kevin Schoonover told me.

 "I can tell you firsthand, shooting missiles off a vessel of that size in sea state two is not easy." The intrigue: A Magnet Defense research craft helped track and evaluate the launch. The company is working with Hanwha to build medium unmanned surface vessels.

 The first prototype is being manufactured now. Go deeper: Navy unveils "ShipOS" with Palantir to speed up shipbuilding 

 
 
