 3. Defense-tech testers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Valkryn exited stealth with $17 million raised and a workforce with ties to some of America's buzziest businesses — including Applied Intuition , Palantir Technologies and SpaceX.

 Why it matters: The Washington-based startup wants to help call the balls and strikes of defense tech by serving as a neutral auditor of the robotic and autonomous systems troops are increasingly wielding (and being threatened by).

 "Test is scarce. It's really hard to do. It's really rare to do," co-founder Hugh Zabriskie told me. "Companies wait months for an opportunity to demonstrate themselves," he said. "There's a lot of talk about being more adaptable and moving faster . Ultimately, that is on the back of test capacity, proving capacity." State of play: Valkryn today is advertising:

 "Battle Lab" technology, which instruments real-world tests of autonomous systems under realistic combat conditions and measures how they perform. Quickly available test records and scoring measured against government criteria to help officials figure out which UAV, UGV or USV is the best fit. "The reason there is often such a large gap between claimed performance — I'm sure you've seen this — and real performance is that there is such a lack of ability to understand that performance in the first place," Zabriskie said.

 "We primarily operate on the fact that the government already has a massive outlay of land and airspace, and this is a resource that is fundamentally underutilized." Between the lines: This all hinges on an unbiased track record — plus test, evaluation and training dollars.

 "Neutral means we don't build the systems that are under test," Zabriskie said. The intrigue: Valkryn recently worked with a task force in the Middle East. Additional details are scarce.

 Follow the money: The startup's $15 million seed was led by Andreessen Horowitz . Ambition Capital, Caffeinated Capital and Cantos Ventures participated.

 It previously raised a $2 million pre-seed. The bottom line: " With this growing ocean of robotic and autonomous systems, we're reaching a breaking point," Zabriskie said.

 "Traditional testing is very event-based and waterfall-based," he added. "The future is very clearly continuous." Go deeper: Musk returns to Trump world for Pentagon war study 

 
 
