 4. AFRICOM evac 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The Merlin Pilot has completed hundreds of autonomous fixed-wing flights. Photo: Courtesy of Merlin

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Merlin will soon participate in an autonomous casualty evacuation demonstration in Morocco, under a $500,000 contract with U.S. Africa Command.

 Why it matters: American military resources on the continent are sparse, especially compared with Europe or the Asia-Pacific .

 Driving the news: Merlin last week announced the run of show, which includes:

 Initial tasking and flight planning Autonomous departure and flight to an "austere casualty collection site" Simulated patient loading An autonomous return flight and landing What they're saying: "The military has a real problem — it needs to move people and supplies over longer distances, often where communications are unreliable, without putting more crews at risk. Autonomy can help solve that," chief executive Matt George told me.

 "This isn't a someday capability. We believe it's ready to prove out now." Zoom in: Merlin will use a Cessna 208B Grand Caravan for the demonstration. 

 It features a large cargo door and a flat-floored cabin, and will be configured with an air ambulance module providing oxygen and power. Friction point: China and Russia have expanded their influence across Africa.

 Chinese, Russian and Iranian warships gathered off South Africa early this year for the Will For Peace naval drills. Meanwhile, the most recent U.S. National Defense Strategy mentioned "Africa" only twice. More from Axios: 

